If this contains source code (real classes, modules, services, or packages), the diagram must depict only those implemented components and how they call, extend, or depend on one another — never mix in project files, configuration files, Dockerfiles, docker-compose files, CI/CD pipeline stages, Makefiles, IaC templates, or other build/deployment tooling as nodes alongside the real code, even if they were discussed in the text above. Never include test files, test suites, mocks, fixtures, or testing frameworks as nodes — the diagram depicts the software's actual functionality, never how it is verified. Prioritize the codebase's own hand-written components; a third-party library or framework class should appear as a node only when it is genuinely central to the functionality being diagrammed (e.g. a base class the application meaningfully extends, or a runtime/framework the application is fundamentally built around) — otherwise leave imported library internals out entirely rather than diagram them on equal footing with the real components. If any of the components are web/UI components assembled from multiple artifacts (e.g. a template, a stylesheet, and a logic file, as with an Angular, Vue, or React component), collapse each one into a single node representing that component as a whole — never split it into separate nodes for its markup, styling, and logic, and never include a purely presentational/styling artifact as its own node. If there is no source code at all and this is purely configuration, build, or deployment tooling, a diagram of how those configuration pieces relate to and depend on each other is fine — it just must not be dressed up as application architecture.
